# workflow.readme
# How the Document Viewer Works — Full Flow (Backend to Frontend)

---

## Overview

```
User opens app
     │
     ▼
Angular calls GET /api/documents
     │
     ▼
Express queries Neon PostgreSQL → returns document list
     │
     ▼
ng-zorro table renders: Name | Type | Date | View (eye icon)
     │
     ▼
User clicks eye icon on a row
     │
     ▼
Angular calls GET /api/preview/:id
     │
     ▼
Express queries DB for supabase_path → creates Supabase signed URL (3600s)
     │
     ▼
Returns JSON { type, title, url }
     │
     ▼
Angular picks the correct viewer based on type:
  pdf / doc / ppt  → viewer="google"
  excel / xlsx     → viewer="office"
  image            → <img> tag directly
     │
     ▼
ngx-doc-viewer renders the file full screen using the signed URL
```

---

## Step 1 — App Loads: Fetch Document List

### Backend — `server.js`
```js
app.get('/api/documents', async (req, res) => {
  const docs = await sql`SELECT id, title, file_type, created_at FROM documents ORDER BY created_at DESC`;
  res.json(docs);
});
```
Queries the Neon PostgreSQL database and returns all documents as JSON.

### Frontend — `app.ts`
```ts
ngOnInit() {
  this.http.get<DocRecord[]>('http://localhost:3000/api/documents').subscribe({
    next: (data) => { this.docs = data; this.tableLoading = false; },
    error: () => { this.tableLoading = false; },
  });
}
```
On component init, calls the backend and stores the list in `docs[]`.

### Frontend — `app.html`
```html
<nz-table [nzData]="docs" [nzLoading]="tableLoading" nzBordered>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name</th>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Date</th>
      <th>View</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    @for (doc of tbl.data; track doc.id) {
      <tr>
        <td>{{ doc.title }}</td>
        <td><nz-tag [nzColor]="tagColor(doc.file_type)">{{ doc.file_type }}</nz-tag></td>
        <td>{{ doc.created_at | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' }}</td>
        <td>
          <button nz-button nzType="link" (click)="openPreview(doc)">
            <span nz-icon nzType="eye"></span>
          </button>
        </td>
      </tr>
    }
  </tbody>
</nz-table>
```
ng-zorro renders the table. Each row has a colored type tag and an eye icon button.

---

## Step 2 — User Clicks Eye Icon: Fetch Signed URL

### Frontend — `app.ts`
```ts
openPreview(doc: DocRecord) {
  this.modalTitle = doc.title;
  this.modalLoading = true;
  this.docUrl = '';

  this.http.get<{ type: string; title: string; url: string }>(
    `http://localhost:3000/api/preview/${doc.id}`
  ).subscribe({
    next: (meta) => {
      this.docType = meta.type;
      this.docViewer = meta.type === 'image' ? 'url'
        : ['excel', 'xlsx', 'xls'].includes(meta.type) ? 'office'
        : 'google';
      this.docUrl = meta.url;
      this.modalLoading = false;
    },
  });
}
```
Calls `/api/preview/:id`. When the response comes back it sets:
- `docUrl` — the Supabase signed URL
- `docType` — the file type (pdf, word, excel, image etc.)
- `docViewer` — which ngx-doc-viewer engine to use

### Backend — `server.js`
```js
app.get('/api/preview/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const docs = await sql`SELECT * FROM documents WHERE id = ${id}`;
  const doc = docs[0];
  const type = resolveType(doc);       // figures out pdf / excel / word / image etc.
  const url = await getSignedUrl(doc); // creates a 3600s Supabase signed URL
  return res.json({ type, title: doc.title, url });
});
```

### How `resolveType` works
```js
function resolveType(doc) {
  const fromColumn = (doc.file_type || '').toLowerCase();
  const fromPath = (doc.supabase_path || '').split('.').pop().toLowerCase();
  const ext = fromPath || fromColumn;
  if (['doc', 'docx', 'word'].includes(ext)) return 'word';
  if (['xls', 'xlsx', 'excel'].includes(ext)) return 'excel';
  if (['ppt', 'pptx'].includes(ext))          return 'ppt';
  if (ext === 'pdf')                           return 'pdf';
  if (['jpg', 'jpeg', 'png', 'gif', 'webp', 'bmp', 'svg', 'image'].includes(ext)) return 'image';
  return ext;
}
```
Checks the file extension from `supabase_path` first, then falls back to the `file_type` column.

### How `getSignedUrl` works
```js
async function getSignedUrl(doc) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase.storage
    .from(process.env.SUPABASE_BUCKET)
    .createSignedUrl(doc.supabase_path, 3600); // expires in 1 hour
  return data.signedUrl;
}
```
Creates a temporary signed URL directly from Supabase Storage. The original file URL is never exposed — only this short-lived signed URL is sent to the frontend.

---

## Step 3 — File Renders Full Screen

### Frontend — `app.html`
```html
@if (docUrl || modalLoading) {
  <div class="viewer-page">

    <!-- Back button toolbar -->
    <div class="viewer-toolbar">
      <button nz-button (click)="closeModal()">← Back</button>
      <span>{{ modalTitle }}</span>
    </div>

    @if (modalLoading) {
      <nz-spin nzTip="Loading document..."></nz-spin>

    } @else {
      <div class="viewer-wrap">

        <!-- blocks Google toolbar download/share buttons -->
        @if (docType !== 'excel') {
          <div class="toolbar-block"></div>
        }

        <!-- image renders as <img> -->
        @if (docType === 'image') {
          <img [src]="docUrl" />

        <!-- all other types use ngx-doc-viewer -->
        } @else {
          <ngx-doc-viewer
            [url]="docUrl"
            [viewer]="docViewer"
            style="width:100%; height:calc(100vh - 56px);"
          ></ngx-doc-viewer>
        }

      </div>
    }
  </div>
}
```

---

## Is `ngx-doc-viewer` Necessary?

**Yes — it is the core rendering engine of the entire viewer.**

Without it, you would need to build your own iframe logic, handle Google Docs viewer URLs manually, manage Office Online viewer URLs, and handle loading/error states yourself.

`ngx-doc-viewer` handles all of this:

| viewer="google" | Wraps the file URL in `https://docs.google.com/viewer?url=...` inside an iframe — works for PDF, DOCX, PPTX |
|---|---|
| viewer="office" | Wraps the file URL in `https://view.officeapps.live.com/op/embed.aspx?src=...` — works for XLSX, DOCX, PPTX |
| viewer="url" | Renders the URL directly in an iframe — used for images |

### Viewer Selection Logic
```ts
this.docViewer = meta.type === 'image'                          ? 'url'
               : ['excel', 'xlsx', 'xls'].includes(meta.type)  ? 'office'
               : 'google';
```

| File Type | Viewer Used | Why |
|---|---|---|
| pdf | google | Google Docs viewer renders PDFs cleanly |
| doc / docx / ppt / pptx | google | Google Docs viewer handles all Office formats |
| xls / xlsx / excel | office | Microsoft Office Online renders Excel better than Google |
| image (jpg/png/gif etc.) | `<img>` tag | No viewer needed — direct image render |

---

## Security Applied

| What | How |
|---|---|
| Ctrl+S, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+U blocked | `@HostListener('document:keydown')` in `app.ts` |
| Google toolbar download/share buttons hidden | `.toolbar-block` div overlays the top-right corner of the iframe |
| Signed URL expires in 1 hour | `createSignedUrl(path, 3600)` in `server.js` |
| Original Supabase file path never sent to browser | Only the signed URL is returned from `/api/preview/:id` |

---

## File Structure (Only What Matters)

```
doc-viewer/                        ← Angular frontend
└── src/app/
    ├── app.ts                     ← Component logic (fetch docs, open preview, viewer selection)
    ├── app.html                   ← Template (ng-zorro table + ngx-doc-viewer full page)
    ├── app.scss                   ← Styles (table, viewer page, toolbar block)
    └── app.config.ts              ← Providers (HttpClient, Animations, ng-zorro icons)

document-viewer/                   ← Express backend
└── server.js                      ← Two API endpoints: /api/documents + /api/preview/:id
```
